Offline Perl regression tests

From the collection root:

    prove -v tools/t/perl-tools.t

Uses only Perl core modules, including Test::More. Commands run in disposable
fixtures. BSD make and HTTP requests are mocked: no ports or Codex are compiled,
no packages are installed, and no external network is accessed. The suite also
loads the sandbox regression driver's private helpers to exercise capture and
failure handling on a Unix host. This does not validate pledge/unveil.

The small programs under `tools/t/fixtures/` exercise the capture helper
without inline Perl (`perl -e`).

To validate the actual sandbox after building/installing Codex on OpenBSD:

    perl devel/codex/files/openbsd-sandbox-regress.pl /usr/local/bin/codex

That separate native suite compiles small C/Rust/Cargo fixtures. It requires
both `openbsd-sandbox-regress.pl` and `openbsd-sandbox-probe.pl`; the driver
copies the probe into its disposable workspace.

The local Perl::Critic installation can be used without persistent changes.
Adjust the paths if your local install uses a different prefix:

    env PATH="$HOME/perl5/bin:$PATH" \
        PERL5LIB="$HOME/perl5/lib/perl5" \
        perlcritic --severity 1 tools/*.pl tools/t/*.t \
        devel/codex/files/*.pl net/simplex-chat/files/*.pl net/simplexmq/files/*.pl

Port build helpers are POSIX shell. Check them with:

    sh -n <helper>...
    shellcheck -s sh <helper>...

No critic policy exclusions are installed. Remaining default-policy findings
are recorded in local maintenance notes under `audit/`, which is not tracked.

## Agent and skill synchronization

Every repository change must include a substantive, relevant agent or skill
instruction update. Run the structural checker from the repository root:

    perl scripts/validate-agent-knowledge.pl --scope worktree

It compares the working tree with `HEAD`; for CI or branch review, use
`--scope range --base origin/main --head HEAD`.
The checker rejects changes without instruction updates and empty/trivial
instruction diffs, then checks skill frontmatter, required maintenance headings,
and local Markdown link targets. It uses Perl core modules and Git only. It
cannot assess semantic relevance, so reviewers must still confirm the changed
instructions teach reusable, accurate knowledge. Run `prove -v
tools/t/perl-tools.t` when changing Perl tools, including this checker.
